; Built around #53's collapsing floor. One long flat strip, all at y=300
; (nobody starts with a height advantage), made of five slabs with 20 px
; seams between them -- narrower than a body, so at round start it reads and
; plays as one continuous floor. Two of the five are collapsing-floor
; instances on each side of a permanent centre, and which trigger goes where
; is the whole idea: the outer slabs are "timed", so the rim gives way on a
; schedule every player can learn and plan around; the inner slabs are
; "stood_on", so the safer ground just outside the permanent core wears out
; specifically under whoever parks on it waiting out the round. The stage
; does not just get smaller -- it punishes camping the edge of safety
; slightly more than camping the middle of it, which is the one thing a
; plain timer alone could not do.
;
; FarLeft/FarRight (timed, 20 s, 1.5 s warning): x=-400..-260 and 260..400.
; Twenty seconds in, both go together (same schedule), and the playable
; strip shrinks from 800 px wide to 480 in one visible step -- well before
; the lava's 50 s grace runs out, so the first squeeze is the floor's own
; and the players feel it coming from the amber shake, not from the rising
; surface. Losing a Far slab costs no one a route: beyond it was already
; open sky over the kill zone, so it only moves the ring-out edge inward.
;
; NearLeft/NearRight (stood_on, 1.5 s to warn): x=-240..-150 and 150..240.
; Immediately outboard of the core, this is the ground a player retreats to
; without committing to the middle -- and exactly the ground that then
; starts ticking under them. Hop off before 1.5 s and it holds forever;
; stand on it and it is gone in under 3 s all told. With four players loose
; on the same strip, that clock is always running on somebody.
;
; Core (permanent): x=-130..130, room for all four spawns with 34 px to
; spare on each side -- comfortably past Erosion's 216 px / 12 px precedent
; for "fits four and nothing more". It is the one thing that cannot go, so
; a round can never deadlock with no floor left anywhere, and it is where
; the fight ends up however the two triggers play out.
;
; Accessibility: if a Near slab goes while its Far neighbour is still there,
; the gap from the core's edge to that Far slab is 130 px -- inside the
; pickaxe's 150 reach with room to spare, a hook the sword (70) and dagger
; (80) cover with a real fling rather than a hook, and trivial for the
; staff. If a Far slab goes first, there is nothing to cross at all: the
; stage has simply gotten narrower, and the core is still 130 px of clear
; approach away with open sky the whole way. No path here ever needs more
; than a hook and a step off the far lip.
;
; Escape: every square inch of this stage is open sky above y=300, so the
; lava (grace 50 s, rise 80 s) never traps anyone -- there is no ceiling and
; no alcove to be caught under, only ground that is or is not there yet.
; By the time the surface is a real threat the floor has usually already
; shrunk once on its own, so the last refuge is the same core a full round
; of erosion cannot take away.

